@page "/help/nav"
@using SpaceSails.Core

@* #949 · HOW A TRIP IS PLANNED, IN EIGHT PICTURES.

   Owner, 2026-08-18, having just flown the scrubbed plan for the first time: "We should have a help
   page where we show multi-step plan and the use of the schrub and burn. New player seeing this image
   would understand how to play. I really like the increment and decrement options."

   Two rules this page is built under:

   1. NO SCREENSHOTS. A picture of the live panel is out of date the afternoon somebody moves a button,
      and nothing fails when it goes stale. Every illustration here is a hand-drawn inline SVG of the
      panel's SHAPE — the scrub bar, a burn row and its ± buttons, the arrive row's ✓/✗ badge. A sketch
      that is obviously a sketch cannot lie about pixels it never claimed to have.

   2. THE BUTTON FACES ARE BORROWED, NEVER TYPED. Every ± face below is the same
      NodeFrame.Nudge*Label call the real toolbar renders, so moving a step constant moves this page
      with it. This repo's named bug class is the sim doing one thing while a sentence reports another;
      a help page is the easiest place in the codebase for that to happen unnoticed.

   The full reference — every desk, every system — stays in the Captain's Guide. This is the two-minute
   read that gets a new captain from "I clicked Plot and nothing happened" to a ship in orbit. *@

<PageTitle>SpaceSails — Plotting a trip</PageTitle>

<div class="container py-4 help-nav-page">
    <div class="d-flex justify-content-between align-items-center mb-3 flex-wrap gap-2">
        <h1 class="mb-0">Plotting a trip — the eight steps</h1>
        <div class="d-flex gap-2">
            <a class="btn btn-outline-dark btn-sm" href="/guide">📖 Full Guide</a>
            <a class="btn btn-outline-dark btn-sm" href="/">← Harbor</a>
        </div>
    </div>

    <p class="text-secondary">
        SpaceSails has honest orbital mechanics, which means you cannot steer to Mars — you
        <em>throw</em> the ship at where Mars is going to be, then check your work. The Nav desk's
        plotting table is where that throwing is done, with the sky frozen and nothing at stake.
        This is the whole loop, once, in order. Two minutes.
    </p>
    <p class="text-secondary">
        <strong>The one idea:</strong> you are never asked to type a trajectory. You add a burn, then
        press <strong>@NodeFrame.NudgeMagnitudeLabel(1, false)</strong> /
        <strong>@NodeFrame.NudgeMagnitudeLabel(-1, false)</strong> and
        <strong>@NodeFrame.NudgeEpochLabel(1, false)</strong> /
        <strong>@NodeFrame.NudgeEpochLabel(-1, false)</strong> and watch the drawn course move under
        your thumb until the last row turns <span class="text-success fw-bold">✓</span>. Iterating is
        the game.
    </p>

    <ol class="list-unstyled help-nav-steps">

        @* ── 1 ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
        <li class="row g-3 align-items-center mb-4">
            <div class="col-md-7">
                <h2 class="h5" id="step-1">1. Pick a target</h2>
                <p>
                    Click a planet, moon or haven on the map — or give the order at the Captain's desk
                    (<kbd>0</kbd>) under <strong>Fly to</strong>. The Nav destination is now set, and
                    everything the plotting table says from here on is measured against it. Without a
                    target you can still plot; you just have nothing to be judged against.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-5">@SketchTarget()</div>
        </li>

        @* ── 2 ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
        <li class="row g-3 align-items-center mb-4">
            <div class="col-md-7">
                <h2 class="h5" id="step-2">2. Press Plot</h2>
                <p>
                    <strong>Plot</strong> sits on the Nav toolbar (or press <kbd>E</kbd> at the NAV POST
                    console inside the ship). The sim <em>pauses</em>. Nothing you do on this table costs
                    fuel or time until you leave it — it is a scratchpad, not the helm.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-5">@SketchToolbar()</div>
        </li>

        @* ── 3 ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
        <li class="row g-3 align-items-center mb-4">
            <div class="col-md-7">
                <h2 class="h5" id="step-3">3. Scrub to a future time</h2>
                <p>
                    Drag the <strong>Scrub</strong> slider and the whole solar system runs forward as a
                    set of <em>ghosts</em>, each tethered to where its body is right now. The scrub is
                    your finger on the plan: it is the moment every "at scrub" button below acts on.
                    Scrub until the target's ghost is somewhere your ship could plausibly meet it.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-5">@SketchScrub()</div>
        </li>

        @* ── 4 ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
        <li class="row g-3 align-items-center mb-4">
            <div class="col-md-7">
                <h2 class="h5" id="step-4">4. + Add burn at scrub</h2>
                <p>
                    This drops a maneuver step onto the plan at the scrubbed moment and opens its editor.
                    Aim it with the four words — <strong>▶ FORWARD</strong>, <strong>◀ BACK</strong>,
                    <strong>▲ UP</strong>, <strong>▼ DOWN</strong> — which are read off your
                    <em>trajectory at that node</em>, not off where the nose points now. A ribbon appears
                    showing where that burn actually sends you.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-5">@SketchAddBurn()</div>
        </li>

        @* ── 5 ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
        <li class="row g-3 align-items-center mb-4">
            <div class="col-md-7">
                <h2 class="h5" id="step-5">5. Iterate with the ± buttons</h2>
                <p>
                    Now the part you will spend the most time on, and the part that needs no keyboard at
                    all. Each burn row carries three pairs:
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>@NodeFrame.NudgeLabel(-1) / @NodeFrame.NudgeLabel(1)</strong> — swing the
                        aim off wherever it points now.</li>
                    <li><strong>@NodeFrame.NudgeMagnitudeLabel(-1, false) /
                        @NodeFrame.NudgeMagnitudeLabel(1, false)</strong> and
                        <strong>@NodeFrame.NudgeMagnitudeLabel(-1, true) /
                        @NodeFrame.NudgeMagnitudeLabel(1, true)</strong> — spend more or fewer pulses.</li>
                    <li><strong>@NodeFrame.NudgeEpochLabel(-1, false) /
                        @NodeFrame.NudgeEpochLabel(1, false)</strong> and
                        <strong>@NodeFrame.NudgeEpochLabel(-1, true) /
                        @NodeFrame.NudgeEpochLabel(1, true)</strong> — fire it earlier or later.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>
                    <strong>The course re-solves under every press.</strong> Watch the
                    <em>closest pass</em> number on the plot card and keep pressing whichever button makes
                    it smaller. The typed fields are still there for fine trims — but they are the
                    exception now, not the way in.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-5">@SketchIterate()</div>
        </li>

        @* ── 6 ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
        <li class="row g-3 align-items-center mb-4">
            <div class="col-md-7">
                <h2 class="h5" id="step-6">6. + Add orbit (or dock) at scrub</h2>
                <p>
                    The plan has to end somewhere. Beside <em>+ Add burn at scrub</em> sit
                    <strong>🛰 + Add orbit at scrub</strong> and <strong>⚓ + Add dock at scrub</strong>,
                    each naming the body it would add. The step they append is the burn rows' twin, with
                    one extra thing on it: a <span class="badge bg-success">✓ VALID</span> /
                    <span class="badge bg-danger">✗ INVALID</span> badge.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>✗ is not a refusal — it is a to-do list.</strong> An invalid arrival says in
                    numbers how far off you are on <em>both</em> gates, distance and closing speed:
                    <em>"pass 3.60 M km, need ≤ 0.50 M km; rel 12.4 km/s, need ≤ 8.0 km/s"</em>. Go back to
                    step 5 and press the ± buttons until it turns ✓. The numbers tell you which way.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-5">@SketchArrive()</div>
        </li>

        @* ── 7 ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
        <li class="row g-3 align-items-center mb-4">
            <div class="col-md-7">
                <h2 class="h5" id="step-7">7. Arm it</h2>
                <p>
                    Open the arrive step and press
                    <strong>🛰 Arm the arrival at <em>Mars</em> — finish the trip from here</strong>. You can
                    do this at <em>plan time</em>, with burns still ahead of you: arming is a decision about
                    the whole trip, not a thing you have to be awake for at the pass. It arms the autopilot
                    for <em>that body, at that pass</em> — never whatever happens to be nearest when the
                    moment comes.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The badge then reads <strong>ARMED</strong>, and the step says
                    <em>"The plan is complete. The autopilot takes her at the pass — nothing more is needed
                    unless something interferes."</em> That is the sentence you are plotting towards.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-5">@SketchArm()</div>
        </li>

        @* ── 8 ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
        <li class="row g-3 align-items-center mb-4">
            <div class="col-md-7">
                <h2 class="h5" id="step-8">8. Warp</h2>
                <p>
                    Leave the plotting table, un-pause, and push the <strong>warp</strong> slider. Burns
                    fire themselves at their planned moments and clean themselves off the card once
                    they are history. Warp drops automatically near a body so nothing important happens
                    at 10 000×. When the arrival fires, the ship parks herself and the trip is over.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-5">@SketchWarp()</div>
        </li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="h5">If it will not converge</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Check the frame.</strong> An Earth→Mars trip is read in the <em>Sun's</em> frame; in
            Earth's frame a perfectly good transfer looks like nonsense. The burn editor names the frame
            it is reading in and offers the trip's frame in one press.</li>
        <li><strong>Move the burn, not the burn size.</strong> Interplanetary trips are a phasing problem
            before they are a fuel problem — @NodeFrame.NudgeEpochLabel(1, true) a dozen times often does
            what @NodeFrame.NudgeMagnitudeLabel(1, true) never will.</li>
        <li><strong>Watch <em>Planned</em> against your reaction mass.</strong> A plan you cannot pay for
            is a plan that strands you.</li>
        <li>Everything else — piracy, trade, the surface game, the electric sky — is in the
            <a href="/guide">Captain's Guide</a>.</li>
    </ul>
</div>

@code {
    // ── THE SKETCHES ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    //
    // Deliberately crude, deliberately inline, deliberately NOT a screenshot. Each one draws the SHAPE
    // a new captain is looking for on the real panel — a slider with a handle, a row of small buttons
    // with units on them, a badge at the right end of a step — at a size that reads on a phone.
    //
    // The dark plate matches the game's own plot panel so the shapes are recognisable when the player
    // switches tabs, and every glyph is drawn with an explicit fill so no theme can wash it out.

    private const string Plate = "#1b1f27";      // the plot panel's dark ground
    private const string Ink = "#e9ecef";        // body text on the plate
    private const string Dim = "#8d97a5";        // secondary text
    private const string Info = "#4dd0e1";       // the ± button ink (btn-outline-info)
    private const string Good = "#2fbf71";       // ✓ VALID
    private const string Bad = "#e0555f";        // ✗ INVALID
    private const string Warn = "#e8b84b";       // the dock/arm accent

    /// <summary>Opening tag for every sketch: one viewBox, scaled to its column, labelled for screen
    /// readers by the caller's own heading.</summary>
    private static MarkupString Frame(string title, string body) => new(
        $"""
        <svg viewBox="0 0 320 120" role="img" aria-label="{title}" class="w-100 help-nav-sketch"
             style="max-width:320px;height:auto;">
          <rect x="0" y="0" width="320" height="120" rx="8" fill="{Plate}"/>
          {body}
        </svg>
        """);

    /// <summary>A small pill button face — the repeated shape in four of the eight sketches.</summary>
    private static string Pill(double x, double y, double w, string face, string ink) =>
        $"""
        <rect x="{x}" y="{y}" width="{w}" height="18" rx="4" fill="none" stroke="{ink}" stroke-width="1"/>
        <text x="{x + w / 2}" y="{y + 13}" fill="{ink}" font-size="10" font-family="monospace"
              text-anchor="middle">{face}</text>
        """;

    private MarkupString SketchTarget() => Frame("A planet on the map, clicked, with a destination reticle on it",
        $"""
        <circle cx="70" cy="60" r="4" fill="{Ink}"/>
        <text x="70" y="82" fill="{Dim}" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle">ship</text>
        <circle cx="235" cy="48" r="9" fill="{Warn}"/>
        <circle cx="235" cy="48" r="17" fill="none" stroke="{Info}" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/>
        <line x1="235" y1="24" x2="235" y2="31" stroke="{Info}" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <line x1="235" y1="65" x2="235" y2="72" stroke="{Info}" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <text x="235" y="90" fill="{Ink}" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle">Mars — destination</text>
        <path d="M78 58 Q150 30 214 46" fill="none" stroke="{Dim}" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 4"/>
        """);

    private MarkupString SketchToolbar() => Frame("The Nav toolbar, with the Plot button on it",
        $"""
        <rect x="14" y="42" width="292" height="36" rx="6" fill="#11151b"/>
        {Pill(24, 51, 46, "Plot", Info)}
        {Pill(78, 51, 52, "Scope", Dim)}
        {Pill(138, 51, 22, "?", Dim)}
        <text x="176" y="64" fill="{Dim}" font-size="10" font-family="monospace">warp  1×</text>
        <text x="24" y="30" fill="{Ink}" font-size="11">Nav desk toolbar</text>
        <text x="24" y="98" fill="{Dim}" font-size="10">press Plot — the sim pauses</text>
        """);

    private MarkupString SketchScrub() => Frame("The Scrub slider with its handle dragged to the right",
        $"""
        <text x="16" y="26" fill="{Ink}" font-size="11" font-family="monospace">Scrub: day 143</text>
        <line x1="16" y1="48" x2="304" y2="48" stroke="{Dim}" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round"/>
        <line x1="16" y1="48" x2="212" y2="48" stroke="{Info}" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round"/>
        <circle cx="212" cy="48" r="8" fill="{Info}"/>
        <text x="16" y="70" fill="{Dim}" font-size="9">now</text>
        <text x="304" y="70" fill="{Dim}" font-size="9" text-anchor="end">+2 y</text>
        <circle cx="60" cy="96" r="4" fill="{Ink}"/>
        <circle cx="150" cy="96" r="4" fill="none" stroke="{Dim}" stroke-dasharray="2 2"/>
        <circle cx="240" cy="96" r="4" fill="none" stroke="{Dim}" stroke-dasharray="2 2"/>
        <text x="150" y="112" fill="{Dim}" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle">ghosts at the scrubbed time</text>
        """);

    private MarkupString SketchAddBurn() => Frame("The + Add burn at scrub button and the step it appends",
        $"""
        {Pill(16, 14, 128, "+ Add burn at scrub", Ink)}
        <rect x="16" y="46" width="288" height="26" rx="4" fill="#11151b"/>
        <circle cx="28" cy="59" r="4" fill="{Info}"/>
        <text x="40" y="63" fill="{Ink}" font-size="10" font-family="monospace">&#9656; FORWARD &#215;6</text>
        <text x="296" y="63" fill="{Dim}" font-size="10" font-family="monospace" text-anchor="end">day 143</text>
        {Pill(16, 80, 60, "&#9654; FWD", Info)}
        {Pill(80, 80, 60, "&#9664; BACK", Dim)}
        {Pill(144, 80, 60, "&#9650; UP", Dim)}
        {Pill(208, 80, 60, "&#9660; DOWN", Dim)}
        """);

    private MarkupString SketchIterate() => Frame("A burn row's three pairs of increment and decrement buttons",
        $"""
        <text x="16" y="20" fill="{Dim}" font-size="9">aim</text>
        {Pill(52, 8, 42, NodeFrame.NudgeLabel(-1), Info)}
        {Pill(98, 8, 42, NodeFrame.NudgeLabel(1), Info)}
        <text x="16" y="52" fill="{Dim}" font-size="9">size</text>
        {Pill(52, 40, 42, NodeFrame.NudgeMagnitudeLabel(-1, true), Info)}
        {Pill(98, 40, 42, NodeFrame.NudgeMagnitudeLabel(-1, false), Info)}
        <rect x="144" y="40" width="34" height="18" rx="4" fill="#11151b" stroke="{Dim}"/>
        <text x="161" y="53" fill="{Ink}" font-size="10" font-family="monospace" text-anchor="middle">6</text>
        {Pill(182, 40, 42, NodeFrame.NudgeMagnitudeLabel(1, false), Info)}
        {Pill(228, 40, 42, NodeFrame.NudgeMagnitudeLabel(1, true), Info)}
        <text x="16" y="84" fill="{Dim}" font-size="9">when</text>
        {Pill(52, 72, 42, NodeFrame.NudgeEpochLabel(-1, true), Info)}
        {Pill(98, 72, 42, NodeFrame.NudgeEpochLabel(-1, false), Info)}
        <text x="161" y="85" fill="{Ink}" font-size="10" font-family="monospace" text-anchor="middle">d143</text>
        {Pill(182, 72, 42, NodeFrame.NudgeEpochLabel(1, false), Info)}
        {Pill(228, 72, 42, NodeFrame.NudgeEpochLabel(1, true), Info)}
        <text x="16" y="110" fill="{Info}" font-size="9">the course re-solves under every press</text>
        """);

    private MarkupString SketchArrive() => Frame("The arrive step, once invalid and once valid",
        $"""
        {Pill(16, 8, 132, "&#128752; + Add orbit at scrub", Info)}
        {Pill(154, 8, 132, "&#9875; + Add dock at scrub", Warn)}
        <rect x="16" y="38" width="288" height="26" rx="4" fill="#11151b" stroke="{Bad}"/>
        <circle cx="28" cy="51" r="4" fill="{Bad}"/>
        <text x="40" y="55" fill="{Ink}" font-size="10" font-family="monospace">orbit at Mars</text>
        <rect x="232" y="43" width="62" height="16" rx="4" fill="{Bad}"/>
        <text x="263" y="55" fill="#11151b" font-size="9" font-family="monospace" text-anchor="middle">&#10007; INVALID</text>
        <text x="40" y="78" fill="{Bad}" font-size="9" font-family="monospace">pass 3.60 M km, need &#8804; 3.00 M km</text>
        <rect x="16" y="86" width="288" height="26" rx="4" fill="#11151b" stroke="{Good}"/>
        <circle cx="28" cy="99" r="4" fill="{Good}"/>
        <text x="40" y="103" fill="{Ink}" font-size="10" font-family="monospace">orbit at Mars</text>
        <rect x="240" y="91" width="54" height="16" rx="4" fill="{Good}"/>
        <text x="267" y="103" fill="#11151b" font-size="9" font-family="monospace" text-anchor="middle">&#10003; VALID</text>
        """);

    private MarkupString SketchArm() => Frame("The arrive step's editor, with the Arm button in it",
        $"""
        <rect x="16" y="12" width="288" height="24" rx="4" fill="#11151b"/>
        <text x="30" y="28" fill="{Ink}" font-size="10" font-family="monospace">&#9662; orbit at Mars</text>
        <rect x="240" y="16" width="54" height="16" rx="4" fill="{Good}"/>
        <text x="267" y="28" fill="#11151b" font-size="9" font-family="monospace" text-anchor="middle">&#10003; VALID</text>
        <rect x="24" y="46" width="272" height="22" rx="4" fill="{Good}"/>
        <text x="160" y="61" fill="#11151b" font-size="10" font-family="monospace" text-anchor="middle">&#128752; Arm the arrival at Mars</text>
        <text x="160" y="90" fill="{Good}" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle">&#8220;the plan is complete&#8221;</text>
        {Pill(24, 100, 176, "scrub to it", Info)}
        {Pill(208, 100, 88, "&#10006; remove", Bad)}
        """);

    private MarkupString SketchWarp() => Frame("The warp slider pushed up, and a burn firing on the ribbon",
        $"""
        <text x="16" y="24" fill="{Dim}" font-size="10" font-family="monospace">warp</text>
        <line x1="56" y1="20" x2="256" y2="20" stroke="{Dim}" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round"/>
        <line x1="56" y1="20" x2="206" y2="20" stroke="{Warn}" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round"/>
        <circle cx="206" cy="20" r="7" fill="{Warn}"/>
        <text x="266" y="24" fill="{Ink}" font-size="10" font-family="monospace">1000&#215;</text>
        <path d="M28 96 Q120 40 292 62" fill="none" stroke="{Info}" stroke-width="2"/>
        <circle cx="28" cy="96" r="4" fill="{Ink}"/>
        <circle cx="120" cy="61" r="4" fill="{Warn}"/>
        <text x="120" y="52" fill="{Warn}" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle">burn fires</text>
        <circle cx="292" cy="62" r="8" fill="{Good}"/>
        <text x="286" y="86" fill="{Ink}" font-size="10" text-anchor="end">in orbit</text>
        """);
}
